  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Testing Time Invariance",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compare the shift-then-system and system-then-shift expressions for an arbitrary input shift to classify a continuous-time input-output system as time invariant or time varying.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Testing Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Testing Time Invariance",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To test time invariance, compare two paths for the same input shift $T$: shift the input first and then apply the system, or apply the system first and then shift the output by the same $T$. The object to recognize is the pair of expressions that should match if the system preserves shifts without changing its rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=T\\{x(t-T)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The first path is the shift-then-system path: start with the shifted input $x(t-T)$, feed it into the system, and name the result $y_1(t)$. For the simplest base case, this is the path that shows what the system does after the delay has already been built into the input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_2(t)=y(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The second path is the system-then-shift path: first compute the output $y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}$, then replace $t$ with $t-T$ to form the shifted output $y_2(t)$. Written directly, this is the same as taking the output rule and evaluating it at the delayed time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_2(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}\\big|_{t\\to t-T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The decision rule is simple: if the simplified expressions for $y_1(t)$ and $y_2(t)$ are exactly the same for every valid $T$, the system is time invariant. In the base identity case $y(t)=x(t)$, both paths reduce to $x(t-T)$, so the matching rule is easy to see before any harder examples appear.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=y_2(t)\\;\\forall T\\;\\Rightarrow\\;\\text{time invariant}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This section sets up the comparison strategy only; later sections will reuse the same notation to test specific system rules and decide whether the paths match or fail to match after simplification.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Testing Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Matching Shift-Then-System and System-Then-Shift Expressions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $y(t)=2x(t)$, compute the shift-then-system and system-then-shift expressions and decide if they are equal.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is a constant-gain system: multiplying by $2$ should not change the shape of the signal when the input is delayed. To test that, start by shifting the input by $T$ and then pass that shifted input through the system.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=2x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That first path is the shift-then-system path. The input becomes $x(t-T)$ first, and the system rule $y(t)=2x(t)$ turns it into $y_1(t)=2x(t-T)$ with the delay preserved inside the argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=2x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now take the system-then-shift path. Apply the system to the original input first, which gives $y(t)=2x(t)$, and then shift that output by the same $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_2(t)=2x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The delayed output has the same simplified form as the shift-then-system path, so the two expressions match exactly: $y_1(t)=y_2(t)=2x(t-T)$. Since the two paths are identical for arbitrary $T$, this system is time invariant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Testing Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Nonmatching Shift-Then-System and System-Then-Shift Expressions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $y(t)=x(t)+t$, compute the shift-then-system and system-then-shift expressions for an arbitrary shift $T$ and identify the term that prevents them from matching.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the explicit $t$ term in the output rule. Start with the shifted input $x(t-T)$, then apply the system rule at time $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=T\\{x(t-T)\\}=x(t-T)+t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the system first, then shift the entire output by $T$. The input part shifts the same way, but the added time term must also be evaluated at the shifted time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_2(t)=\\bigl(T\\{x(t)\\}\\bigr)\\big|_{t\\to t-T}=x(t-T)+(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compare the two expressions term by term. The input-dependent parts match, but the explicit-time terms differ: $t$ in $y_1(t)$ and $t-T$ in $y_2(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)-y_2(t)=T",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the two paths do not match for arbitrary $T$, the system is time varying.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Testing Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Testing Equality for an Arbitrary Input Shift",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $y(t)=t\\,x(t-2)$, simplify both paths $y_1(t)=T\\{x(t-T)\\}$ and $y_2(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}|_{t\\to t-T}$ to test equality.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the time prefactor $t$ multiplying the shifted input $x(t-2)$. Start by shifting the input first, then apply the system rule at time $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=t\\,x((t-T)-2)=t\\,x(t-T-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the system first, then shift the whole output by $T$. The shifted output inherits the same input argument change, but the prefactor also shifts from $t$ to $t-T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_2(t)=\\bigl(t\\,x(t-2)\\bigr)\\big|_{t\\to t-T}=(t-T)x(t-T-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compare the two expressions term by term. The shifted input argument matches, but the coefficients do not: $t$ in $y_1(t)$ versus $t-T$ in $y_2(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)-y_2(t)=T\\,x(t-T-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the two paths disagree for arbitrary $T$, this system is time varying.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Testing Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Classifying Time Invariance from Simplified Path Expressions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Use the following path expressions and classify the system: $y_1(t)=x(t-T+1)$, $y_2(t)=x(t-T+1)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that both paths are already simplified. At this point, the job is to compare the final symbolic forms for exact equality for arbitrary $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=x(t-T+1),\\qquad y_2(t)=x(t-T+1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the two expressions term by term. The shifted argument is identical, and there are no extra coefficients or time offsets that change one path relative to the other.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=y_2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the simplified path expressions match exactly for arbitrary $T$, the system is time invariant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=y_2(t)\\ \\text{for all }T\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\text{time invariant}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the decision rule for the lesson: if the two simplified paths are identical for every input shift, classify the system as time invariant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "question_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Testing Time Invariance",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compare the shift-then-system and system-then-shift expressions for an arbitrary input shift to classify a continuous-time input-output system as time invariant or time varying.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Testing Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Matching Shift-Then-System and System-Then-Shift Expressions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=3x(t)$ with input shift $x_1(t)=x(t-T)$, which comparison correctly classifies the system?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1(t)=3x(t-T)$, $y_2(t)=3x(t-T)$, and therefore $y_1(t)=y_2(t)$; the system is time invariant.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1(t)=3x(t+T)$, $y_2(t)=3x(t-T)$; this mismatch in shift direction means the paths do not match.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1(t)=3x(t)$ and $y_2(t)=3x(t-T)$; the unshifted path would be skipped in this test.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1(t)=3x(t-T)$, $y_2(t)=3x(t-T+1)$; these expressions are not equal.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_2(t)=3x(t-T)$ and no $y_1(t)$ is needed because this is a gain-only system.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "Choice A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Shift first gives $x_1(t)=x(t-T)$ and then applying $3\\cdot$ gives $y_1(t)=3x(t-T)$. Shifting the original output $y(t)=3x(t)$ by $T$ gives $y_2(t)=3x(t-T)$. Both sides match for arbitrary $T$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=5x(t-1)$ and $x_1(t)=x(t-T)$, identify the correct path comparison and conclusion.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1(t)=5x((t-T)-1)=5x(t-T-1)$ and $y_2(t)=5x((t-T)-1)=5x(t-T-1)$, so they are equal and the system is time invariant.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1(t)=5x((t+T)-1)=5x(t+T-1)$, $y_2(t)=5x((t-T)-1)$, so the advance/de-delay mix causes a mismatch.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1(t)=5x((t-T)-1)$ and only this path is required for the time-invariance test.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1(t)=5x(t-1-T)$, $y_2(t)=5x(t-1-T)-T$; therefore the system is not time invariant.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1(t)=5x(t-1)$, $y_2(t)=5x((t-T)-1)$; because only $y_2$ contains $T$, the system is time varying.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "Choice A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Start from $y(t)=5x(t-1)$. Shift first gives $x_1(t)=x(t-T)$ and $y_1(t)=5x_1(t-1)=5x((t-T)-1)=5x(t-T-1)$. Shift second gives $y_2(t)=y(t-T)=5x((t-T)-1)=5x(t-T-1)$. The expressions are identical, so the system is time invariant.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Testing Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Nonmatching Shift-Then-System and System-Then-Shift Expressions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=x(t)+2t$, use the same shift test to classify whether the system is time invariant.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Let $y_1(t)=T\\{x(t-T)\\}$ and $y_2(t)=y(t-T)$, then choose the statement that matches this computation.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "display-math",
                "text": "",
                "latex": "y_1(t)=T\\{x(t-T)\\}=x(t-T)+2t",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "display-math",
                "text": "",
                "latex": "y_2(t)=y(t-T)=x(t-T)+2(t-T)",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "The expressions do not match: $y_1(t)=x(t-T)+2t$ and $y_2(t)=x(t-T)+2(t-T)$. The system is time varying.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "The expressions match exactly: $y_1(t)=x(t-T)+2t= y_2(t)=x(t-T)+2(t-T)$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
